          %p
            The first drawing corresponds to the picture as a (2,3)-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torus+knot"}> torus knot
            \. The knot thus can be represented on the surface of a solid torus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1\times D^2
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^3
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^2\times S^1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^2
            in each case is the corresponding
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            of the other case. Thinking of the trefoil on this other solid torus it is a
            = succeed "-torus" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 3
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (3,2)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≤
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b(T_{2,3})\leq 2
          %p
            %em
              How does one know that the trefoil does not have
              = succeed "?" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi K
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b(K) = 1
          %p For that we use
          .un_proposition
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              A knot
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              has
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b(K) = 1
              if and only if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              is the unknot.
          %p
            To complete the proof that
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi trefoil
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b(trefoil)= 2
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                colorability
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/n-colorability"} ?
          .un_proposition
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              The bridge number is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot+invariant"} knot invariant
          %p
            This is more or less ‘by default’ as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b(K)
            is defined as a minimum over all
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot+diagram"} knot diagrams
          %h5#remark Remark:
          %p
            Why is there the convention that
            = succeed "?" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi unknot
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b(unknot)= 1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prime+knot"} prime knots
            then you can (theoretically) calculate it for all composite knots. This states the following:
          .un_proposition
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              be two (oriented) knots, and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo +
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K+L
              be their oriented
              = succeed "," do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  sum
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/knot+sum"} ?
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi K
                    %mo +
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b(K+L) = b(K) + b(L) -1.
          %p
            The proof is not hard. As ‘
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            + unknot =
            = succeed "\u2019," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
          %p
            (The pictures of the trefoil on this page are based on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/two-trefoils.pdf", :title => "Picture showing the usual 3-bridge and the less well known 2-bridge versions of the trefoil"} this picture of two trefoils
            from the
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://www.popmath.org.uk/exhib/knotexhib.html"} Maths and Knots Exhibition website
        .revisedby
